// `print` does not append a newline; `println` does. Exactly one.
//
// Both backends once printed through a shared format table whose every entry
// ended in "\n", so `print` emitted a newline it does not have: on the Rust
// backend these four calls ran onto four lines while the native backend put
// them on one. `println` looked right by accident, because the newline it
// wanted happened to already be in the format string.
//
// Nothing catches that from the outside except looking at the bytes, which is
// why the expectation is the exact run of bytes rather than a line count. The
// one newline below is println(7)'s; none of the print calls contributes one,
// so the "|" that follows 7 starts its own line.
//
// Expected: ab|7
// Expected: |2.5|
fn main() {
    print("a");
    print("b");
    print("|");
    println(7);
    print("|");
    // 2.5, not 2.500000: every float crosses the runtime boundary as an
    // IEEE-754 bit pattern and is printed by ionic_print_float64, which formats
    // with "%g". A backend using "%lf" prints 2.500000 here and fails.
    print(2.5);
    println("|");
}
